WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.900
Hi folks. My name is Alex Sharma.

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:09.260
I'm a developer advocate at CircleCI and I am here today to talk to you about Continuous Integration.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:14.400
Which is a software development practice where everyone on the engineering team

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.560
is continuously integrating these small code changes back into the codebase.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.820
After each change that they're making, there's a suite of tests that runs automatically

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.320
that checks the code for any bugs or errors or anything like that.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:33.930
So what does that look like in practice? I've drawn this beautiful flow chart up here for you.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.080
Step one is you make a change.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:41.180
And this is something maybe on Github. You make a future branch, you push it up, submit a pull request.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.169
That's the change that I'm talking about here.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:48.239
Push that up, then you run your tests automatically, and that's that's pretty important

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:54.149
The tests need to be run in a consistent way so that everyone on your team has full confidence that

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:56.700
they're running the tests in the same way.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:01.320
You don't really have a guarantee that your machine is the same as your neighbor's machine.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:07.020
That's really hard to to lock down. So those tests are running automatically somewhere else.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:13.160
If they pass, you're good to go and you can just keep making changes.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:17.920
You go right back around, submit another pull request. Go wild.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.439
If the tests fail, you need to make sure. It's your responsibility to fix the code.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.080
Fix the code until your tests pass and then you can continue developing.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:33.660
So it's this wonderful cycle. The idea being that you want to catch these bugs as soon as you can.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.700
So they're not lingering over a long period of time.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.500
So that's what Continuous Integration is and next time, we'll talk about why you would be doing this.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:43.700
We'll dig into that. Thanks!

